// Runtime/ManagedFileRuntime.maxon — the `__ManagedFile` box: one OS file HANDLE, closed on drop (R4.1).
//
// ⭐ **THE ORACLE IS `specs/managed-file.md`, AND IT IS THE ONLY ORACLE AVAILABLE FOR THIS SUBSYSTEM.**
// Both references implement file IO — the bootstrap in `2-Parser.cs:1414-1440`, v1 in
// `LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1801-1842` — and they AGREE on the ~13-method surface, so there is no divergence
// to resolve there. What is NOT available is the usual ad-hoc question: `run_program compiler=csharp`
// CRASHES (`E9001`, index out of range in `MaxonToStandardConversion`) on any standalone `__ManagedFile.*`
// call, and the bootstrap's own suite is green over it only because its runner BATCHES the cases. That is
// filed in PLAN.md's bootstrap-oracle list. ⇒ **The 11 committed cases are the whole oracle; a question the
// corpus does not already ask cannot be asked of the bootstrap at all.** Prefer reading v1's lowering over
// inventing an answer, and say so at the site when neither can settle something.
//
// ⭐ **HOST-ONLY BY RULING (2026-07-30).** `x64-windows` is real; every other target refuses at the call
// site with the EXISTING `E3104 targetUnsupportedConstruct` via `targetHasWin32Substrate`
// (`SemanticCheck.maxon`), which is the same route that makes the wall clock host-only. No new mechanism,
// and deliberately NOT a per-backend port: v1's ~3,900 lines of per-backend emission buy lanes the oracle
// cannot even validate (the bootstrap has no Linux or wasm emitter), and its arm64 directory walk is
// unfinished in a way that returns an empty result with no error.
//
// ⚠ **DO NOT PORT v1's HANDLE BLOCK BY INSPECTION.** Both references REGISTER an 8-byte handle struct
// while v1's real `__ManagedDirectory` runtime block is 40 bytes, with nothing tying the two together —
// a type-checking fiction beside a runtime shape. Here the two are held together by the drift check in
// `SignatureIndex.managedFileLayout`, which panics if this file's offsets and that layout disagree.

// ⭐ **THE BOX'S FIELD OFFSET AND ITS SIZE** — asserted against the registered `StructLayout`
// (`SignatureIndex.managedFileLayout`) rather than agreeing with it by inspection, exactly as
// `CharSetCharsOffset`/`CharSetBoxBytes` did for the `CharacterSet` box until W129 retired that pair with
// its runtime — **this is the LAST such pair, so the discipline has no second example left to copy**: the
// SHAPE is stated for the front end by that layout and
// written here by these constants, and two readers of one shape is the pair this project keeps finding
// drifted.
//
// One 8-byte slot holding the raw OS HANDLE. `specs/managed-file.md`'s "Type Structure" names the single
// `_handle` field, and the corpus never reads it — direct construction is refused (E3072) so the handle
// cannot be forged, and every read is the runtime's own through this offset.
export let ManagedFileHandleOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
export let ManagedFileBoxBytes = 8 as ParsedInt

// The handle value a CLOSED box carries. `close()` is documented idempotent and is ALSO called by the
// destructor, so the two must be able to run in either order and in both orders — `managed-file.exists`
// closes explicitly and then lets the struct drop, which is exactly that sequence. Storing this sentinel
// on close is what makes the second close a no-op rather than a double `CloseHandle` on a reused handle.
//
// ⚠ It is `0`, not Win32's `INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE` (-1), and the distinction is deliberate: a box is
// allocated zeroed, so an unopened box and a closed box carry the same value and one guard covers both.
// A handle the OS actually returns is never 0 for a file.
export let ManagedFileClosedHandle = 0 as ParsedInt

// `CreateFileA` reports failure as `INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE`, which is -1 and NOT the zero a closed box
// carries. Two distinct sentinels for two distinct facts: "the OS refused" vs "this box holds no handle".
export let InvalidHandleValue = -1 as ParsedInt

// The Win32 last-error codes the corpus discriminates. `specs/managed-file.md`'s own note records that
// Win32 `ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND` and POSIX `ENOENT` are BOTH 2 — which is why the errno→variant mapping is
// builder-built Std here rather than backend code, and why a future POSIX lane reuses this arithmetic
// unchanged and supplies only the OPEN.
let ErrorFileNotFound = 2 as ParsedInt
let ErrorPathNotFound = 3 as ParsedInt
let ErrorAccessDenied = 5 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **THE `__ManagedFileError` ORDINALS, AS ERROR FLAGS (`ordinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias`).** DERIVED from
// the bias rather than spelled 1…11, exactly as `ManagedMemoryRuntime.ArrayErrorIndexOutOfBoundsFlag` is — and the ordinals
// themselves are positional facts about the case order of the enum DECLARED at `stdlib/Builtins.maxon:175`
// (A1s). `verifyManagedFileOrdinals` below is what holds these literals to that declaration.
//
// ⚠ `renameFailed` is ABSENT because the enum has no such case: both references route a failed rename to
// `deleteFailed` (`MaxonToStandardConversion.ManagedFile.cs:321-323`), and inventing a case here would put
// a variant in shv2's enum that no `match` in the corpus can name.
let ManagedFileNotFoundOrdinal = 0 as ParsedInt
let ManagedFileAccessDeniedOrdinal = 1 as ParsedInt
let ManagedFileOpenFailedOrdinal = 2 as ParsedInt
let ManagedFileReadFailedOrdinal = 3 as ParsedInt
let ManagedFileWriteFailedOrdinal = 4 as ParsedInt
let ManagedFileSizeFailedOrdinal = 5 as ParsedInt
let ManagedFileDeleteFailedOrdinal = 6 as ParsedInt
let ManagedFileStatFailedOrdinal = 7 as ParsedInt
// ⚠ **8 AND 9 CARRY NO FLAG BECAUSE NOTHING THROWS THEM, AND THE ORDINALS ARE HERE ANYWAY (W135).**
// `invalidStatBuffer` and `invalidStatIndex` name the two ways a `statField`/`statFree` call can be handed
// something impossible, and shv2 answers both with a runtime ABORT rather than a throw
// (`buildManagedFileStatField`'s two guards → `RuntimeAbort.managedFileStatNullBuffer` /
// `managedFileStatIndexOutOfRange`, and `buildManagedFileStatFree`'s → the first of those). The buffer is
// the runtime's own; a corpus program never forges one, so a caller reaching either guard has a compiler
// defect and not a recoverable failure. The two positions are transcribed regardless, because they are what
// puts `closed` at 10 — and until W135 they were the gap a corpus reorder could move in silence.
let ManagedFileInvalidStatBufferOrdinal = 8 as ParsedInt
let ManagedFileInvalidStatIndexOrdinal = 9 as ParsedInt
let ManagedFileClosedOrdinal = 10 as ParsedInt
let ManagedFileNotFoundFlag = ManagedFileNotFoundOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedFileAccessDeniedFlag = ManagedFileAccessDeniedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedFileOpenFailedFlag = ManagedFileOpenFailedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedFileReadFailedFlag = ManagedFileReadFailedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedFileWriteFailedFlag = ManagedFileWriteFailedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedFileSizeFailedFlag = ManagedFileSizeFailedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedFileDeleteFailedFlag = ManagedFileDeleteFailedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedFileStatFailedFlag = ManagedFileStatFailedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedFileClosedFlag = ManagedFileClosedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias

// ⭐ **`WIN32_FIND_DATAA` — the 320-byte record `FindFirstFileA` fills**, which `stat` and `exists` read the
// filesystem through and which `__ManagedDirectory` iterates over (R4.3). The offsets are the Win32 struct's,
// transcribed once (the bootstrap transcribes the same six at `X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:3777-3786` and
// `cFileName` at `:2359`). Three of them are NOT 8-aligned (`ftCreationTime` at 4, `ftLastAccessTime` at 12,
// `ftLastWriteTime` at 20) because the record begins with a DWORD; x64 loads them unaligned without ceremony.
//
// ⚠ The buffer is HEAP, not stack: the Std tier has no stack-slot primitive, so `stat`/`exists` allocate
// it through `__mm_alloc` and free it before returning — on EVERY path, including the failing one. A
// directory search keeps its record for the LIFETIME of the search instead, inside its find block.
//
// ⚠ **THE SIZE AND THE OFFSETS ARE ONE FACT WITH TWO FAMILIES READING IT, WHICH IS WHY THEY ARE EXPORTED
// FROM HERE RATHER THAN RE-TRANSCRIBED (R4.3).** A second copy of `cFileName is at 44` is a number nothing
// checks: get it wrong and `filename()` returns a slice of a timestamp, silently.
export let FindDataBytes = 320 as ParsedInt
let FindDataAttributesOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
let FindDataCreationTimeOffset = 4 as ByteOffset
let FindDataLastAccessTimeOffset = 12 as ByteOffset
let FindDataLastWriteTimeOffset = 20 as ByteOffset
let FindDataSizeHighOffset = 28 as ByteOffset
let FindDataSizeLowOffset = 32 as ByteOffset

// `cFileName` — a NUL-terminated `char[MAX_PATH]` at the record's tail. Read only by `__ManagedDirectory`
// (R4.3): `stat` and `exists` are told the path they asked about, so neither has any use for it.
export let FindDataFileNameOffset = 44 as ByteOffset

// The two `dwFileAttributes` bits the corpus can observe: `exists` answers 0 for a DIRECTORY (the spec
// says "Returns 1 if the file exists (and is not a directory)"), and `stat` publishes both as 0/1.
export let FileAttributeDirectory = 0x10 as ParsedInt
let FileAttributeReadOnly = 0x1 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **THE 48-BYTE STAT BUFFER — six i64 fields, packed exactly as the bootstrap packs them**
// (`X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:3815-3872`). `stat` returns this buffer's ADDRESS as a bare `int`, the caller
// reads a field with `statField(buffer, index)` and MUST hand it back to `statFree(buffer)`; the field
// ORDER is therefore a wire format between the three, exactly as the error-enum's case order is.
//
// ⚠ The two attribute fields are **0 or 1**, never the raw mask — `statField(buf, 4)` answers "is it a
// directory?", not "what is bit 4 of dwFileAttributes?".
let StatBufferFields = 6 as ParsedInt
let StatFieldBytes = 8 as ParsedInt
let StatBufferBytes = StatBufferFields * StatFieldBytes
let StatSizeOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
let StatModifiedOffset = 8 as ByteOffset
let StatCreatedOffset = 16 as ByteOffset
let StatAccessedOffset = 24 as ByteOffset
let StatIsDirectoryOffset = 32 as ByteOffset
let StatIsReadOnlyOffset = 40 as ByteOffset

// FILETIME counts 100 ns intervals since 1601-01-01 UTC; Unix time counts seconds since 1970-01-01. The
// delta is the number of 100 ns intervals between those two epochs, and both references spell exactly
// these two numbers.
let FileTimeUnixEpochDelta = 116444736000000000 as ParsedInt
let FileTimeTicksPerSecond = 10000000 as ParsedInt

// `nFileSizeHigh`/`nFileSizeLow` are two DWORDs; the size is `(high << 32) | low`.
let FileSizeHighShift = 32 as ParsedInt

// ⚠ **THE Std TIER HAS NO 4-BYTE MEMORY ACCESS, so a DWORD is read as a WORD and masked.**
// `accessWidthFor` carries `byte` and `word64` and PANICS for everything between — deliberately, because a
// narrow load's SIGNEDNESS is a decision it refuses to guess (see its own comment). Every DWORD this file
// reads is unsigned, so a full-word load plus `and 0xFFFFFFFF` is the honest spelling and needs no new
// access width: the four extra bytes are always inside the 320-byte find record.
export let DwordMask = 0xFFFFFFFF as ParsedInt

// The `__mf_` prefix that identifies EVERY file-IO runtime entry point — the one test `scanRuntimeUsage`
// reads to set `usesManagedFile` (and `usesHeap`), and the one `SemanticCheck` reads for E3104.
let ManagedFileRuntimePrefix = b"__mf_"

// The Win32 BOOL a failed call returns. Every file import except the two opens and `FindFirstFileA`
// answers with one, and the test is always "is it zero?".
export let Win32False = 0 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **THE SCRATCH WORD A Win32 OUT-PARAMETER IS WRITTEN THROUGH** — `GetFileSizeEx`'s `PLARGE_INTEGER` and
// `ReadFile`/`WriteFile`'s `lpNumberOfBytes*`. Named for THAT purpose rather than reusing `StatFieldBytes`
// (which is a stat record's field STRIDE): the two are both 8 and mean different things, and one constant
// standing for two facts is what a later change to either would silently move.
//
// ⚠ **8 BYTES FOR A 4-BYTE WRITE IS DELIBERATE, AND ITS CORRECTNESS RESTS ON THE SLAB'S ZEROING CONTRACT.**
// `lpNumberOfBytesRead`/`Written` are `LPDWORD` — the OS writes FOUR bytes — yet the count is read back as a
// full i64. That is right because `__mm_alloc` hands out zeroed memory and says so (see its refcount
// comment: the zeroing is the allocator's stated guarantee, not a local accident), so the upper four bytes
// are 0 and the word IS the DWORD. Masking here would be the caller re-establishing a guarantee the
// allocator already makes — the precise habit `MmRuntime` warns against.
let Win32OutWordBytes = 8 as ParsedInt

// Where the OUT-PARAMETER's answer sits inside that word: at the front of it, because the word IS the answer.
// ⭐ Named rather than spelled `0 as ByteOffset` at each read — three files reach `emitWin32OutWord`'s block
// (here, `ConsoleRuntime`, `ManagedSocketRuntime`) and a bare literal at each of them is the SAME fact about
// this allocation's layout, transcribed three times, in three files, none of which declares it.
export let Win32OutWordAnswerOffset = 0 as ByteOffset

// ⭐ **THE THIRTEEN ENTRY POINTS.** Twelve name a `__ManagedFile` method; `__mf_destruct` is the
// compiler-INSERTED one — the drop a `__ManagedFile` box takes when its last owner goes away
// (`SignatureIndex.managedNameDropCallee`), which is the whole of the RAII half of this type.
//
// ⚠ **THERE IS NO `__mf_open_write_exec`, AND THAT IS THE `openWriteExecutable` DECISION, NOT AN
// OMISSION.** The only thing that method adds in either reference is a Unix 0755 permission bit, and shv2's
// file IO is host-only Windows by the R4.1 ruling — so the parser's `openWriteExecutable` arm emits
// `__mf_open_write` and the two are the same call. A POSIX lane splits them on the day it exists.
export let ManagedFileOpenReadName = b"__mf_open_read"
export let ManagedFileOpenWriteName = b"__mf_open_write"

// The close. NON-throwing, which is what makes the drift guard below a real check rather than a
// tautology: thirteen callees, eight throwing and five not, and the predicate must separate them.
export let ManagedFileCloseName = b"__mf_close"
export let ManagedFileDestructName = b"__mf_destruct"
export let ManagedFileSizeName = b"__mf_size"
export let ManagedFileReadName = b"__mf_read"
export let ManagedFileWriteName = b"__mf_write"
export let ManagedFileExistsName = b"__mf_exists"
export let ManagedFileDeleteName = b"__mf_delete"
export let ManagedFileRenameName = b"__mf_rename"
export let ManagedFileStatName = b"__mf_stat"
export let ManagedFileStatFieldName = b"__mf_stat_field"
export let ManagedFileStatFreeName = b"__mf_stat_free"

// ⭐ **WHICH `__ManagedFile` CALLEES THROW** — the third composer behind `Parser.isThrowingRuntimeCallee`,
// beside `isThrowingManagedMemoryRuntimeCallee` and `isThrowingGtRuntimeCallee`. Held to the builders by the
// bidirectional drift guard in `installManagedFileRuntime`, which is copied from `installManagedMemoryRuntime`
// deliberately: a predicate and a `throws` stamp are one fact in two places, and this project keeps
// finding that pair drifted.
export function isThrowingManagedFileRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	// ⚠ **THE FIVE ABSENTEES ARE EACH ABSENT FOR A STATED REASON, not by oversight.**
	//   • `__mf_close` — the spec documents `close()` as "Does not throw"; naming it here would make a `try`
	//     required on a call that cannot fail (E3055), which the drift guard below caught on first write.
	//   • `__mf_destruct` — a DROP has no caller to hand an error to; it runs at a scope exit the user did
	//     not write.
	//   • `__mf_exists` — 0 is a valid answer ("it does not exist"), not a failure, so there is nothing to
	//     throw. Both references register it non-throwing for exactly that reason.
	//   • `__mf_stat_field` / `__mf_stat_free` — stdlib-only INVARIANT sites. A null buffer or an index past
	//     the six fields is a caller bug, not a runtime condition, so they ABORT (see `RuntimeAbort`) rather
	//     than hand back an error a caller would have to pretend to handle. Both references do the same.
	return callee.equals(ManagedFileOpenReadName) or callee.equals(ManagedFileOpenWriteName) or callee.equals(ManagedFileSizeName) or callee.equals(ManagedFileReadName) or callee.equals(ManagedFileWriteName) or callee.equals(ManagedFileDeleteName) or callee.equals(ManagedFileRenameName) or callee.equals(ManagedFileStatName)
end 'isThrowingManagedFileRuntimeCallee'

// ⭐ **WHAT THE USER ACTUALLY WROTE (D12)** — this family's `managedDirectorySourceMethodName`, and read
// through the same composer (`GtRuntime.runtimeCalleeSourceMethod`). Until D12 an `__ManagedFile.openRead(p)`
// written without a `try` was told it was a "throwing array accessor": the right E3057, naming a construct
// the program does not contain.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A MAP ONTO THE PARSER'S OWN CONSTANTS, NOT A SECOND SPELLING** — each arm answers with the very
// `ManagedFile*Method` byte string `parseManagedFileStaticCall`/`dispatchManagedFileMethod` dispatched on, so
// nothing here can disagree with what the source was matched against. Asked only on the COLD path, and only
// behind `isThrowingManagedFileRuntimeCallee`, which is what the panic states rather than answers.
//
// ⚠⚠ **AS OF D11c IT HAS A SECOND READER, AND THE `delete`/`rename` ARMS ARE ITS ALONE (D11c review).**
// `Parser`'s E3059 asks this same map for a VALUELESS `try`, and `__mf_delete`/`__mf_rename` are the family's
// two void throwing members — so those arms have no E3057 case and E3059 is the only thing that can observe
// them. `specs-shv2/void-call-result.md` pins both there rather than here, beside the other seven callees
// E3059 can reach, because the roster is "throwing AND void" and that crosses every family. **MEASURED: with
// the `delete` arm answering `stat`, the whole suite read 2897/0 while an author who wrote `delete` was told
// about `'stat'`.** ⚠ It follows that this map is asked BEFORE `installManagedFileRuntime`'s drift guard can
// run on an E3059 path — the parse refuses the program first — so the guard covers a missing arm and the
// spec cases cover a WRONG one.
//
// ⚠⚠ **`__mf_open_write` IS A LOSSY KEY, SO IT ANSWERS WITH BOTH SPELLINGS RATHER THAN GUESSING ONE.**
// `openWrite` and `openWriteExecutable` are ONE call at this rung — the executable form adds only a Unix
// 0755 bit, shv2's file IO is host-only Windows by the R4.1 ruling, and the parser's `openWriteExecutable`
// arm therefore emits `__mf_open_write` (see the entry-point block above). The callee carries no evidence of
// which word was typed, so `found(ManagedFileOpenWriteMethod)` would tell an author who wrote
// `openWriteExecutable` to look for an `openWrite` their program does not contain: D12's own defect, one
// level in. `sharedByTwoSpellings` is true whichever was written. ⚠ **This is the ONE arm that is not
// injective, and it is why this map answers the UNION where its three siblings answer a NAME** — a POSIX
// lane that splits the two entry points collapses this arm back to a `found` at no extra cost, and nothing
// else here changes.
export function managedFileSourceMethod(callee ByteArray) returns RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod
	if callee.equals(ManagedFileOpenReadName) 'openRead'
		return RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod.found(ManagedFileOpenReadMethod)
	end 'openRead'
	if callee.equals(ManagedFileOpenWriteName) 'openWrite'
		return RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod.sharedByTwoSpellings(ManagedFileOpenWriteMethod, alsoSpelled: ManagedFileOpenWriteExecutableMethod)
	end 'openWrite'
	if callee.equals(ManagedFileSizeName) 'size'
		return RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod.found(ManagedFileSizeMethod)
	end 'size'
	if callee.equals(ManagedFileReadName) 'read'
		return RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod.found(ManagedFileReadMethod)
	end 'read'
	if callee.equals(ManagedFileWriteName) 'write'
		return RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod.found(ManagedFileWriteMethod)
	end 'write'
	if callee.equals(ManagedFileDeleteName) 'delete'
		return RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod.found(ManagedFileDeleteMethod)
	end 'delete'
	if callee.equals(ManagedFileRenameName) 'rename'
		return RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod.found(ManagedFileRenameMethod)
	end 'rename'
	if callee.equals(ManagedFileStatName) 'stat'
		return RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod.found(ManagedFileStatMethod)
	end 'stat'
	panic("managedFileSourceMethod: no source-method arm for '{String.from(callee)}'. EITHER it is not a throwing `__ManagedFile` callee — this map is asked ONLY behind `isThrowingManagedFileRuntimeCallee`, so nothing else can reach an E3057 — OR that predicate gained a name and this map did not, in which case add an arm answering the parser's own `ManagedFile*Method` constant. `installManagedFileRuntime`'s drift guard asks this for every throwing `__mf_` builder it installs, so the second case fails at INSTALL and never in front of a user's missing `try`")
end 'managedFileSourceMethod'

// ⭐ **THE ERROR TYPE A FILE CALLEE THROWS — the WHAT to the predicate's THAT.** `ProgramSignatures.throwsOf`
// answers with it for a callee no file declares (R4.1), which is what types an `otherwise (e)` binding and
// therefore what makes `match e` see an enum instead of a scalar; the eight throwing builders stamp it as
// their `throws` clause. ONE speller for one fact — kept beside the predicate deliberately, because the two
// are halves of it and the drift guard in `installManagedFileRuntime` holds the predicate to the stamps.
//
// ⚠ It is EXPORTED and there is no `…RuntimeThrowsClause` wrapper beside it any more. R4.1 had one, returning
// a `ThrowsClauseOption` — an optional over a type that ALREADY has a `none` arm — and review measured what
// that cost when a second family joined it: composing two of those optionals put **+20 allocations at rung 0
// DOUBLING to +640 at rung 5** on the ladder, one extra 16-byte union box per `try` in the program. The
// composer (`GtRuntime.runtimeThrowsClause`) now asks the two PREDICATES and returns a `ThrowsClause`
// directly, so exactly one clause value is built per ask, as before. (`docs/optimization-log.md` already
// records the identical signature once — `joinCommaSeparated` in `checkEnumExhaustive`, same
// +20-doubling-to-+640 — under the heading *a dedup that is correct can still be unscalable*.)
export function managedFileThrows() returns ThrowsClause
	return ThrowsClause.throwing(String.from(ManagedFileErrorTypeName))
end 'managedFileThrows'

// ⭐⭐ **THE PATH → NUL-TERMINATED SCRATCH COPY — ONE HOME, SIX CALLERS.**
//
// ⚠ **THE PATH IS COPIED, AND IT HAS TO BE.** Every Win32 path entry point takes an `LPCSTR`, but an
// Array's buffer carries no terminator: only STRING LITERALS are NUL-terminated (in their rdata blob,
// `StringRuntime.stringBlobBytes`), and `"…".toByteArray()` MATERIALIZES a fresh array whose buffer is
// exactly `length` bytes. Writing a NUL at `buffer[length]` in place would be a one-byte heap overflow
// whenever capacity equals length — which is precisely what a freshly materialized view has.
// ⇒ allocate `length + 1`, copy, terminate. The copy reuses `ManagedMemoryRuntime.emitForwardCopy` rather than
// growing a second byte loop. **The caller owns the scratch and must `__mm_free` it on every path.**
//
// Emits into `cur`, routes the copy loop's exit into `join`, and writes the terminator in `join` — so the
// caller carries on from `join` with the pointer this returns. `cur` dominates `join`, which is what lets
// `len` be read in one and used in the other with no block argument.
export function emitPathToCString(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, join IrBlock, pathRec ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let len = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: pathRec, offset: ManagedRecordLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let buf = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: pathRec, offset: ManagedRecordBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let size = emitBin(module, block: cur, a: len, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let scratch = emitRawAlloc(module, block: cur, byteCount: size, ids: ids)
	let copied = emitForwardCopy(module, func: func, cur: cur, dest: scratch, src: buf, byteCount: len, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(copied, op: StdOp.branch(join.id))

	let nulAddr = emitBin(module, block: join, a: scratch, b: len, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let nul = emitConst(module, block: join, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(join, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(nulAddr, offset: 0, valueId: nul, valueType: StdType.u8))
	return scratch
end 'emitPathToCString'

// ⭐ **THE ONE `__mm_alloc` CALL THIS FILE MAKES.** `__mm_alloc(size, destructor)` takes TWO arguments and
// the destructor is always `TrivialDestructor` here — none of the three raw blocks this file allocates (a
// path scratch, a `WIN32_FIND_DATAA`, a stat buffer) owns anything to cascade to.
//
// ⚠ **R4.1 CALLED IT WITH ONE ARGUMENT**, so the destructor slot of both its allocations was whatever
// happened to be in the second argument register. Nothing reads that slot back TODAY (`__mm_free`'s
// destructor cascade is deferred — see its comment), which is why a green suite never noticed; the day it
// does, every `__ManagedFile` box would `callIndirect` through a garbage pointer. Stated once here so the
// arity cannot be got wrong per call site.
export function emitRawAlloc(module StdModule, block IrBlock, byteCount ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let dtor = emitConst(module, block: block, value: TrivialDestructor, ids: ids)
	let ptr = ids.mint()
	emitMmAllocCall(module, block: block, result: ptr, size: byteCount, destructor: dtor, tag: mmTagIndex(MmTag.ElementBuffer), ids: ids)
	return ptr
end 'emitRawAlloc'

// Release one of this file's raw blocks. A `void` result the caller discards, spelled once so the minted
// throwaway id is not repeated at a dozen sites.
export function emitRawFree(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ptr ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: MmFreeName, args: argsOf(ptr)))
end 'emitRawFree'

// One `Win32OutWordBytes` scratch word for an OUT-PARAMETER — see that constant for the size and for why the
// four bytes `ReadFile` does not write read back as zero. The caller owns it and releases it through
// `emitWin32BoolOutcome`.
//
// ⚠ EXPORTED for `ConsoleRuntime` (W5), which hands `osReadFile` an out word for a read on the STDIN handle
// rather than on a `__ManagedFile` box. Exported rather than re-implemented for the reason the constant
// itself is named: the width AND the "the allocator zeroed it, so the four bytes the OS does not write read
// back as 0" guarantee are one fact, and a second copy would be free to drift from either half.
export function emitWin32OutWord(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let wordSize = emitConst(module, block: block, value: Win32OutWordBytes, ids: ids)
	return emitRawAlloc(module, block: block, byteCount: wordSize, ids: ids)
end 'emitWin32OutWord'

// ⭐⭐ **THE TAIL OF EVERY "BOOL VERDICT PLUS AN OUT-PARAMETER WORD" CALL — ONE HOME, THREE CALLERS**
// (`size`, `read`, `write`). The op itself differs in arity and operands and so stays at the call site; what
// follows it does not, and was written out three times: capture the last error, read the answer the OS wrote,
// release the scratch, test the BOOL, and route to the shared errno classification or to the success exit.
//
// ⚠ **THE ORDER OF THE FIRST THREE IS LOAD-BEARING, WHICH IS THE REASON TO STATE IT ONCE.** `osLastError`
// comes IMMEDIATELY after the call — a `__mm_free` in between would run Win32 code of its own and could
// overwrite the thread's last-error — and the READ of the out word comes before the FREE, which is what makes
// the scratch released on BOTH exits rather than on one of them. Three copies of that ordering is three
// chances to get one of them wrong, and the failure would be a wrong errno variant or a leak, not a
// compile error.
//
// `failed` and `ok` arrive from the caller rather than being minted here so the function's block ORDER is the
// caller's to state — it is what the emitted code's layout follows.
function emitWin32BoolOutcome(module StdModule, func IrFunction, block IrBlock, verdict ValueId, out ValueId, failed IrBlock, ok IrBlock, fallbackFlag ParsedInt, ids ValueMinter)
	let err = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.osLastError(err))
	let answer = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: out, offset: Win32OutWordAnswerOffset, ids: ids)
	emitRawFree(module, block: block, ptr: out, ids: ids)
	let falseConst = emitConst(module, block: block, value: Win32False, ids: ids)
	let failedCall = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: verdict, b: falseConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.condBranch(failedCall, thenBlockId: failed.id, elseBlockId: ok.id))

	emitFileErrnoClassifiedThrow(module, func: func, block: failed, err: err, fallbackFlag: fallbackFlag, ids: ids)
	emitOkReturn(module, block: ok, value: answer, ids: ids)
end 'emitWin32BoolOutcome'

// ⭐⭐ **THE errno → VARIANT MAPPING, AND THE ONLY PLACE IT LIVES.** `err` is a `GetLastError` reading the
// caller captured; this turns it into one of THREE error-enum cases and THROWS from `block`.
//
// `ERROR_PATH_NOT_FOUND` joins `ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND` in the `notFound` arm: a missing DIRECTORY component
// is a missing path as far as the corpus is concerned, and `managed-file.open-read-not-found-variant` would
// otherwise fall to the catch-all for a path whose parent does not exist. Anything else takes
// `fallbackFlag` — the per-OPERATION catch-all (`openFailed` for an open, `readFailed` for a read, and so on).
//
// ⚠ **THE THREE FLAGS ARE ALL PARAMETERS, AND R4.3 IS WHY.** `__ManagedDirectory` runs the identical
// classification into `__ManagedDirectoryError`, whose `notFound`/`accessDenied` happen to sit at the same
// two ordinals `__ManagedFileError`'s do (both references order both enums that way). Hard-coding this
// file's two and letting the directory family inherit them would make that coincidence load-bearing and
// silent: reorder either enum and every directory throw would name the wrong case with nothing failing.
// Passed explicitly, the coincidence is a fact each caller states about its OWN enum.
//
// ⚠ It is builder-built Std rather than backend code because the arithmetic is the same on every platform
// even though the READ is not: `specs/managed-file.md`'s own note records that Win32 `ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND`
// and POSIX `ENOENT` are BOTH 2, so a future POSIX lane reuses this unchanged and supplies only the calls.
export function emitErrnoClassifiedThrow(module StdModule, func IrFunction, block IrBlock, err ValueId, notFoundFlag ParsedInt, deniedFlag ParsedInt, fallbackFlag ParsedInt, ids ValueMinter)
	var checkPath = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfchkp")
	var checkDenied = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfchkd")
	var notFoundBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfnf")
	var deniedBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfden")
	var otherBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfoth")

	let notFoundConst = emitConst(module, block: block, value: ErrorFileNotFound, ids: ids)
	let isNotFound = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: err, b: notFoundConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.condBranch(isNotFound, thenBlockId: notFoundBlock.id, elseBlockId: checkPath.id))

	let pathNotFoundConst = emitConst(module, block: checkPath, value: ErrorPathNotFound, ids: ids)
	let isPathNotFound = emitCmp(module, block: checkPath, a: err, b: pathNotFoundConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkPath, op: StdOp.condBranch(isPathNotFound, thenBlockId: notFoundBlock.id, elseBlockId: checkDenied.id))

	let deniedConst = emitConst(module, block: checkDenied, value: ErrorAccessDenied, ids: ids)
	let isDenied = emitCmp(module, block: checkDenied, a: err, b: deniedConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkDenied, op: StdOp.condBranch(isDenied, thenBlockId: deniedBlock.id, elseBlockId: otherBlock.id))

	emitErrorReturn(module, block: notFoundBlock, flag: notFoundFlag, ids: ids)
	emitErrorReturn(module, block: deniedBlock, flag: deniedFlag, ids: ids)
	emitErrorReturn(module, block: otherBlock, flag: fallbackFlag, ids: ids)
end 'emitErrnoClassifiedThrow'

// The three-flag classification, with THIS family's two fixed cases supplied. Every `__ManagedFile` caller
// throws `__ManagedFileError`, so its `notFound`/`accessDenied` are a per-FILE fact rather than a per-call
// one; only the catch-all differs. Not a thin wrapper: it is what keeps the pairing out of five call sites.
function emitFileErrnoClassifiedThrow(module StdModule, func IrFunction, block IrBlock, err ValueId, fallbackFlag ParsedInt, ids ValueMinter)
	emitErrnoClassifiedThrow(module, func: func, block: block, err: err, notFoundFlag: ManagedFileNotFoundFlag, deniedFlag: ManagedFileAccessDeniedFlag, fallbackFlag: fallbackFlag, ids: ids)
end 'emitFileErrnoClassifiedThrow'

// ⚠ The two throwing exits this file used to spell for itself (`emitManagedFileThrow`/`emitManagedFileOk`)
// are `MmRuntime.emitErrorReturn`/`emitOkReturn` — the identical three-line bodies `ManagedMemoryRuntime` also had.
// R4.3 would have been the third copy; see their header in `MmRuntime` for why they now have one home.

// ⭐⭐ **THE LIVE OS HANDLE OF A RECORD, OR THIS FAMILY'S `closed` THROW — ONE HOME FOR THE GUARD ITSELF.**
// A handle-owning box carries its family's CLOSED SENTINEL both before an open and after a close (one
// sentinel, two facts — see `ManagedFileClosedHandle`), so ONE test covers "never opened" and "already
// closed", and operating on either is exactly what a `closed` case names. Emits the test into `entry` and
// routes the live path to `join`; the handle is defined in `entry`, which dominates `join`.
//
// ⚠ **THE PROTOCOL IS WHAT IS SHARED, NOT THE CONSTANTS** (R4.3). `__ManagedFile`'s `size`/`read`/`write`
// and `__ManagedDirectory`'s `next` had this same six-line body twice, differing only in which record the
// handle sits in, which sentinel means closed, and which enum's `closed` flag to throw — all four now
// parameters. The part that was copied is the part that is easy to get silently wrong: which way round the
// `condBranch` goes, and that the throw block is a SEPARATE block dominated by `entry`. Swap the two edges
// in one copy and the other keeps working, while that family's guard stops firing — measured, on the
// directory side, as a **0xC0000005 access violation** from `FindNextFileA(NULL)`, not as a test that
// merely returns the wrong number.
//
// ⚠ The `closed` block's LABEL is deliberately family-neutral and NOT a parameter: labels are diagnostic
// strings only (identity is `block.id`), and `emitErrnoClassifiedThrow` — shared by both families since
// R4.3 — already emits `mf`-prefixed labels inside directory functions.
export function emitLiveHandleOrClosedThrow(module StdModule, func IrFunction, entry IrBlock, join IrBlock, record ValueId, handleOffset ByteOffset, closedSentinel ParsedInt, closedFlag ParsedInt, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let handle = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: record, offset: handleOffset, ids: ids)
	let closedConst = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: closedSentinel, ids: ids)
	let isClosed = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: handle, b: closedConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	var closedBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"closedthrow")
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isClosed, thenBlockId: closedBlock.id, elseBlockId: join.id))
	emitErrorReturn(module, block: closedBlock, flag: closedFlag, ids: ids)
	return handle
end 'emitLiveHandleOrClosedThrow'

// The guard with THIS family's three fixed facts supplied — the handle lives in the box itself, at
// `ManagedFileHandleOffset`. Every `__ManagedFile` caller throws `__ManagedFileError.closed`, so those three
// are a per-FILE fact rather than a per-call one. Not a thin wrapper: it is what keeps the triple out of
// `size`, `read` and `write`.
function emitOpenHandleOrThrow(module StdModule, func IrFunction, entry IrBlock, join IrBlock, box ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitLiveHandleOrClosedThrow(module, func: func, entry: entry, join: join, record: box, handleOffset: ManagedFileHandleOffset, closedSentinel: ManagedFileClosedHandle, closedFlag: ManagedFileClosedFlag, ids: ids)
end 'emitOpenHandleOrThrow'

// The BYTE capacity/length of an `Array` record: the bytes its element count occupies at `element_size@24`.
// Both the read's bound check and the write's length go through it, so the "an Array counts ELEMENTS and a
// file counts BYTES" conversion is written once. (The bootstrap compares a byte count against an ELEMENT
// capacity, which is right only for the element size 1 its corpus always passes; this is the same answer
// there and the correct one everywhere else.)
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE CONVERSION IS `ManagedMemoryRuntime.emitElementByteLen` AND NOT A MULTIPLY, BECAUSE `element_size@24` IS
// SIGNED.** A sub-byte-packed element stamps a NEGATIVE bit width, so `count · es` is a negative extent —
// and this one does not merely compute a wrong number, it becomes `osReadFile`/`osWriteFile`'s LENGTH, where
// DWORD truncation reads it as an enormous unsigned count.
//
// ⚠ **EXPORTED FOR THE SOCKET FAMILY**, whose `sendFrom` bounds `offset + length` against the same byte
// CAPACITY and whose `recv` fills up to it. The conversion — and the SIGNED `element_size@24` trap above —
// is one fact about the `Array` record, not one about files.
//
// ⚠ **NO PACKED RECORD CAN REACH HERE TODAY, AND THAT IS EXACTLY WHY THIS SITE ASKS RATHER THAN ASSUMES.**
// Both callers are gated by `Parser.requireManagedMemoryValue` → `valueIsByteArray` →
// `ProgramSignatures.isBytePackedArrayInstance`, which is `element_size == 1` and so false for every packed
// stride. That is a real guarantee — and it lives in two OTHER files, where nothing points at this
// arithmetic. Asking the shared formula costs one call, is byte-identical for every positive stride, and
// does not depend on a front-end predicate staying exactly as strict as it is now.
export function emitArrayByteExtent(module StdModule, block IrBlock, arrRec ValueId, offset ByteOffset, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let count = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: arrRec, offset: offset, ids: ids)
	let es = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: arrRec, offset: ManagedRecordElementSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	return emitElementByteLen(module, block: block, count: count, es: es, ids: ids)
end 'emitArrayByteExtent'

// ⭐ **WHICH OPEN A BUILDER EMITS.** A bare bool at `buildManagedFileOpen` would be a magic value at both
// call sites; this names the two the corpus has.
enum ManagedFileOpenMode
	read
	write
end 'ManagedFileOpenMode'

// `__mf_open_read(pathRec) -> (box, flag)` / `__mf_open_write(pathRec) -> (box, flag)` — open a file, or
// throw `__ManagedFileError`. ONE builder: the two differ only in which Std op they emit and which
// catch-all variant a non-errno failure takes, so the scratch copy, the failure classification and the box
// are written once.
function buildManagedFileOpen(module StdModule, name ByteArray, mode ManagedFileOpenMode)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: managedFileThrows())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var afterCopy = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfterm")
	var failed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mffail")
	var opened = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfok")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let pathRec = 0 as ValueId

	let scratch = emitPathToCString(module, func: func, cur: entry, join: afterCopy, pathRec: pathRec, ids: ids)

	// Open, capture the last error, then release the scratch IMMEDIATELY — the OS has copied the path by
	// the time `CreateFileA` returns, and holding it until the error paths merge would leak on three of
	// them. The error is captured BEFORE the free (rather than re-read on the failure branch) so nothing
	// between the call and the classification can be allowed to disturb it.
	let handle = ids.mint()
	emitIoPark(module, block: afterCopy, operation: GtIoOperation.fileExists, ids: ids)
	match mode 'op'
		read then module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.osFileOpenRead(handle, pathPtrId: scratch))
		write then module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.osFileOpenWrite(handle, pathPtrId: scratch))
	end 'op'
	let err = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.osLastError(err))
	emitRawFree(module, block: afterCopy, ptr: scratch, ids: ids)
	let invalidConst = emitConst(module, block: afterCopy, value: InvalidHandleValue, ids: ids)
	let isInvalid = emitCmp(module, block: afterCopy, a: handle, b: invalidConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterCopy, op: StdOp.condBranch(isInvalid, thenBlockId: failed.id, elseBlockId: opened.id))

	emitFileErrnoClassifiedThrow(module, func: func, block: failed, err: err, fallbackFlag: ManagedFileOpenFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	// The box: one 8-byte slot holding the handle, allocated through `__mm_alloc` so the ordinary ownership
	// machinery drops it — and the drop it takes is `__mf_destruct`, which CLOSES the handle before freeing
	// the memory (`SignatureIndex.managedNameDropCallee`). That is the whole RAII half of this type.
	let boxSize = emitConst(module, block: opened, value: ManagedFileBoxBytes, ids: ids)
	let box = emitRawAlloc(module, block: opened, byteCount: boxSize, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(opened, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(box, offset: ManagedFileHandleOffset, valueId: handle, valueType: StdType.i64))
	emitOkReturn(module, block: opened, value: box, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedFileOpen'

// `__mf_close(box)` — close the handle the box carries, idempotently. Returns nothing and THROWS nothing:
// `specs/managed-file.md` documents `close()` as "Idempotent. Also called automatically via destructor.
// Does not throw."
//
// ⭐ **IDEMPOTENCE IS THE WHOLE BODY.** The box is zeroed at allocation and this writes
// `ManagedFileClosedHandle` back after closing, so an unopened box and an already-closed box carry the
// same value and ONE guard covers both — which is what makes the documented "close then let the
// destructor close again" sequence safe rather than a double `CloseHandle` on a handle the OS may have
// reused for something else.
function buildManagedFileClose(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedFileCloseName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var doClose = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfdo")
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfdone")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let box = 0 as ValueId

	let handle = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: box, offset: ManagedFileHandleOffset, ids: ids)
	let closedConst = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: ManagedFileClosedHandle, ids: ids)
	let alreadyClosed = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: handle, b: closedConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(alreadyClosed, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: doClose.id))

	emitIoPark(module, block: doClose, operation: GtIoOperation.fileExists, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(doClose, op: StdOp.osFileClose(handle))
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: doClose, value: ManagedFileClosedHandle, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(doClose, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(box, offset: ManagedFileHandleOffset, valueId: zero, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(doClose, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedFileClose'

// ⭐⭐ **`__mf_destruct(box)` — THE RAII DESTRUCTOR (R4.2), AND THE REASON A `__ManagedFile` MAY BE DROPPED
// AT ALL.** The refcount-checked teardown a `__ManagedFile` box takes instead of the generic `__mm_decref`:
// last owner ⇒ CLOSE the handle, then free the eight bytes; otherwise lower the count and keep the box.
//
// ⭐ **AN OS HANDLE IS NOT MEMORY, WHICH IS WHY THIS EXISTS RATHER THAN A SYNTHESIZED CASCADE.** `_handle`
// is a scalar `int` field, so `structFieldIsManaged` is false and `installStructDestructors` skips the
// type outright; and `emitNullGuardedDrop`'s shape — load the FIELD, call `callee(field)` — is wrong for a
// close, which needs the BOX (it zeroes `_handle` to stay idempotent). Widening "managed field" to cover a
// handle would be a lie every other reader of that predicate would then act on. So the drop ROUTER answers
// this name (`SignatureIndex.managedNameDropCallee`, whose other arm is `__ManagedDirectory`'s — a
// `CharacterSet` ⇒ `__cs_decref` arm stood beside them until W129) and the teardown lives here.
//
// ⭐ It reuses `__mf_close` rather than re-emitting the close, so idempotence is stated once: an explicit
// `f.close()` followed by the scope-exit drop finds the sentinel already stored and does nothing —
// `managed-file.exists` runs exactly that sequence, and `managed-file.auto-close` runs the other one (no
// explicit close at all, the handle reclaimed only by this).
//
// The skeleton is `MmRuntime.emitFieldCascadeDestructor`'s, minus the field walk: `emitRefcountCheckToLastOwner`
// is shared with it, so "how many owners are left?" is asked one way by every destructor in the compiler.
function buildManagedFileDestruct(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedFileDestructName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var lower = module.addBlock(func, label: b"lower")
	var drop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"drop")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let box = 0 as ValueId

	emitRefcountCheckToLastOwner(module, func: func, entry: entry, lower: lower, record: box, lastOwner: drop, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(drop, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: ManagedFileCloseName, args: argsOf(box)))
	emitRawFree(module, block: drop, ptr: box, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(drop, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedFileDestruct'

// `__mf_size(box) -> (size, flag)` — `GetFileSizeEx`, or `sizeFailed`/an errno variant.
//
// The 64-bit answer arrives through an out-param (a size cannot share RAX with a success flag), so a
// scratch word is allocated for it and released before either exit.
function buildManagedFileSize(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedFileSizeName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: managedFileThrows())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var doSize = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfsz")
	var failed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mffail")
	var ok = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfok")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let box = 0 as ValueId
	let handle = emitOpenHandleOrThrow(module, func: func, entry: entry, join: doSize, box: box, ids: ids)

	let out = emitWin32OutWord(module, block: doSize, ids: ids)
	let verdict = ids.mint()
	emitIoPark(module, block: doSize, operation: GtIoOperation.fileExists, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(doSize, op: StdOp.osFileSize(verdict, handleId: handle, sizeOutPtrId: out))
	emitWin32BoolOutcome(module, func: func, block: doSize, verdict: verdict, out: out, failed: failed, ok: ok, fallbackFlag: ManagedFileSizeFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedFileSize'

// `__mf_read(box, arrRec, size) -> (bytesRead, flag)` — read up to `size` BYTES into the managed buffer.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`size > capacity` THROWS `readFailed`; IT IS NOT CLAMPED.** The bootstrap documents that exact
// choice at its own pre-check ("a silent clamp in the runtime would hide a user contract violation"), and
// `specs/managed-file.md` states it as the method's contract. A NEGATIVE `size` is refused by the same
// test: `ReadFile`'s `nNumberOfBytesToRead` is a DWORD, so a negative i64 would arrive as an enormous
// unsigned count and read off the end of the buffer.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE BOUND IS THE RECORD'S BYTE CAPACITY, AND IT CANNOT REFUSE A NON-OWNED BUFFER — THIS HEADER
// CLAIMED THE OPPOSITE, AND THE CLAIM WAS FALSE FOR AS LONG AS IT STOOD.** It said a zero-copy view or an
// `.rdata`-backed literal "FAILS IT STRUCTURALLY", because such a record carries a NEGATIVE `capacity@16`
// (the `BufferOwnership` encoding) and every positive `size` is therefore above it. The negative never
// reaches the comparison: `emitArrayByteExtent` → `emitElementByteLen` → `emitBitsToBytes` ends in a
// LOGICAL shift right, so `capacity = -1` arrives as `0x1FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF` — enormous and POSITIVE, and
// every `size` below 2^61 is UNDER it and accepted. **MEASURED on this tree before the fix: a 24-byte
// `read` into a 4-byte zero-copy view over a heap `Array` was ACCEPTED, answered `bytesRead = 24`, and
// `ReadFile` rewrote the parent's four bytes plus 20 more past the end of its allocation.** The identical
// premise was copied into the socket runtime's `sendFrom` and caught there the same way.
//
// ⇒ **THE OWNERSHIP QUESTION IS ASKED BY ITS OWN TEST, AND IT IS `emitRefuseNonOwnedFillTarget` —
// `emitBufferNotOwned`'s guard, shared with `__ms_recv`, which fills a caller's buffer from the kernel for
// the same reasons.** A `read` is a WRITE into memory the caller handed in: of the four non-owned
// provenances one is a READ-ONLY `.rdata` image page and the other three are bytes somebody else is still
// reading. Only a buffer the record OWNS may be filled — `__ManagedMemory.create`'s, or one a mutator has
// already detached.
//
// ⭐ **AND THE REFUSAL IS THE DOCUMENTED CONTRACT RATHER THAN A NEW RULE.** `specs/managed-file.md` says
// *"throws `readFailed` if `size > managed.capacity`"*, and `managed.capacity()` hands `capacity@16` to
// user code unchanged (`__managed_capacity`) — so for a non-owned record the observable capacity is a
// NEGATIVE sentinel and EVERY `size >= 0` is above it, `0` included. That is exactly what this guard now
// does. The byte conversion stays for the OWNED bound, where an Array counts ELEMENTS and a file counts
// BYTES (`emitArrayByteExtent`); what it may not also do is answer the ownership question.
function buildManagedFileRead(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedFileReadName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: managedFileThrows())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var checkLow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfchklo")
	var checkOwned = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfchkown")
	var checkCap = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfchkcap")
	var tooBig = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfbig")
	var doRead = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfrd")
	var failed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mffail")
	var ok = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfok")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let box = 0 as ValueId
	let arrRec = 1 as ValueId
	let size = 2 as ValueId
	let handle = emitOpenHandleOrThrow(module, func: func, entry: entry, join: checkLow, box: box, ids: ids)

	let zero = emitConst(module, block: checkLow, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let negative = emitCmp(module, block: checkLow, a: size, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkLow, op: StdOp.condBranch(negative, thenBlockId: tooBig.id, elseBlockId: checkOwned.id))

	// The sign test reads only the ARGUMENT, so it comes first; the ownership test reads the RECORD and must
	// clear before the capacity below is treated as a count at all. All three leave through one exit — the
	// enum has a single `readFailed` variant and none of the three is a different complaint to a caller.
	emitRefuseNonOwnedFillTarget(module, block: checkOwned, record: arrRec, refuse: tooBig, fill: checkCap, ids: ids)

	// The buffer is OWNED here, so `capacity@16` is a real count and its byte extent is non-negative.
	let byteCapacity = emitArrayByteExtent(module, block: checkCap, arrRec: arrRec, offset: ManagedRecordCapacityOffset, ids: ids)
	let overCapacity = emitCmp(module, block: checkCap, a: size, b: byteCapacity, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkCap, op: StdOp.condBranch(overCapacity, thenBlockId: tooBig.id, elseBlockId: doRead.id))

	emitErrorReturn(module, block: tooBig, flag: ManagedFileReadFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: doRead, record: arrRec, offset: ManagedRecordBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let out = emitWin32OutWord(module, block: doRead, ids: ids)
	let verdict = ids.mint()
	emitIoPark(module, block: doRead, operation: GtIoOperation.fileExists, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(doRead, op: StdOp.osReadFile(verdict, handleId: handle, bufId: buffer, lenId: size, bytesOutPtrId: out))
	emitWin32BoolOutcome(module, func: func, block: doRead, verdict: verdict, out: out, failed: failed, ok: ok, fallbackFlag: ManagedFileReadFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedFileRead'

// `__mf_write(box, arrRec) -> (bytesWritten, flag)` — write the WHOLE live buffer.
//
// ⚠ There is no length PARAMETER, and that is the reference's signature rather than a simplification: the
// method writes `managed`'s live extent, so the byte count is what `length@8` elements occupy at
// `element_size@24` (`emitArrayByteExtent`), read off the record. A caller that wants fewer bytes calls `setLength` first, which is exactly what
// `managed-file.write-and-read` does on the read side.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT DELIBERATELY HAS NO `emitRefuseNonOwnedFillTarget`, AND THAT IS NOT THE READ PATH'S OMISSION.**
// The two differ in the DIRECTION of the transfer, which is the whole of it: `read` hands the kernel a
// pointer to WRITE THROUGH, so a buffer the record does not own is a read-only image page or somebody
// else's live bytes; `write` hands it a pointer to READ FROM, and every provenance's bytes are readable —
// that is what makes a byte-string literal or a zero-copy view a legal thing to write out at all. Refusing
// non-owned here would refuse `File.writeText("…")`, whose content buffer is a view over the literal's
// blob, and take `file-io.md` red with it.
//
// ⭐ **AND ITS EXTENT IS `length@8`, WHICH IS NEVER A SENTINEL.** The negative values the `BufferOwnership`
// encoding uses live in `capacity@16` alone; `length@8` is a real element count for an owner, for a view
// (its window), for an inline String and for an `.rdata` blob. So the logical-shift trap that made the read
// path's capacity bound useless has no counterpart here — this site reads the one field that cannot carry a
// provenance marker.
function buildManagedFileWrite(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedFileWriteName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: managedFileThrows())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var doWrite = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfwr")
	var failed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mffail")
	var ok = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfok")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let box = 0 as ValueId
	let arrRec = 1 as ValueId
	let handle = emitOpenHandleOrThrow(module, func: func, entry: entry, join: doWrite, box: box, ids: ids)

	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: doWrite, record: arrRec, offset: ManagedRecordBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let byteLength = emitArrayByteExtent(module, block: doWrite, arrRec: arrRec, offset: ManagedRecordLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let out = emitWin32OutWord(module, block: doWrite, ids: ids)
	let verdict = ids.mint()
	emitIoPark(module, block: doWrite, operation: GtIoOperation.fileExists, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(doWrite, op: StdOp.osWriteFile(verdict, handleId: handle, bufId: buffer, lenId: byteLength, bytesOutPtrId: out))
	emitWin32BoolOutcome(module, func: func, block: doWrite, verdict: verdict, out: out, failed: failed, ok: ok, fallbackFlag: ManagedFileWriteFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedFileWrite'

// `__mf_exists(pathRec) -> 0 | 1` — NON-throwing: "it does not exist" is an answer, not a failure.
//
// ⭐ **IT ASKS `FindFirstFileA`, THE OP `stat` NEEDS ANYWAY, AND READS THE DIRECTORY BIT OUT OF THE SAME
// RECORD.** The spec's answer is "1 if the file exists AND IS NOT A DIRECTORY", so the find record's
// `dwFileAttributes` is not an accident of the implementation — it is the second half of the question.
// Importing `GetFileAttributesA` (which is what the bootstrap's POSIX-shaped sync worker uses) would be a
// second entry point answering a question this record already answers, and two can disagree.
function buildManagedFileExists(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedFileExistsName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var afterCopy = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfterm")
	var missing = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfmiss")
	var found = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mffound")
	var isDir = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfdir")
	var isFile = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mffile")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let pathRec = 0 as ValueId

	let scratch = emitPathToCString(module, func: func, cur: entry, join: afterCopy, pathRec: pathRec, ids: ids)
	let dataSize = emitConst(module, block: afterCopy, value: FindDataBytes, ids: ids)
	let findData = emitRawAlloc(module, block: afterCopy, byteCount: dataSize, ids: ids)
	let search = ids.mint()
	emitIoPark(module, block: afterCopy, operation: GtIoOperation.fileExists, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.osFileFindFirst(search, pathPtrId: scratch, findDataPtrId: findData))
	emitRawFree(module, block: afterCopy, ptr: scratch, ids: ids)
	let invalidConst = emitConst(module, block: afterCopy, value: InvalidHandleValue, ids: ids)
	let notFound = emitCmp(module, block: afterCopy, a: search, b: invalidConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterCopy, op: StdOp.condBranch(notFound, thenBlockId: missing.id, elseBlockId: found.id))

	emitRawFree(module, block: missing, ptr: findData, ids: ids)
	let zeroAnswer = emitConst(module, block: missing, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(missing, op: StdOp.ret(zeroAnswer))

	// Read the attributes BEFORE the record is released; close the search handle either way — a leaked
	// `FindFirstFileA` handle is the same class of leak the whole of R4.1 exists to avoid.
	let attrs = emitLoadDword(module, block: found, record: findData, offset: FindDataAttributesOffset, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(found, op: StdOp.osFileFindClose(search))
	emitRawFree(module, block: found, ptr: findData, ids: ids)
	let dirMask = emitConst(module, block: found, value: FileAttributeDirectory, ids: ids)
	let dirBit = emitBin(module, block: found, a: attrs, b: dirMask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	let noBit = emitConst(module, block: found, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let directory = emitCmp(module, block: found, a: dirBit, b: noBit, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(found, op: StdOp.condBranch(directory, thenBlockId: isDir.id, elseBlockId: isFile.id))

	let dirAnswer = emitConst(module, block: isDir, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(isDir, op: StdOp.ret(dirAnswer))
	let fileAnswer = emitConst(module, block: isFile, value: 1, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(isFile, op: StdOp.ret(fileAnswer))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedFileExists'

// `__mf_delete(pathRec)` / `__mf_rename(oldRec, newRec)` — VOID and throwing. One builder: both copy their
// path arguments into NUL-terminated scratch, make one Win32 call whose BOOL says whether it worked, and
// classify a zero through the shared errno mapping. Only the OP and the catch-all variant differ, and
// `rename`'s catch-all is `deleteFailed` because the enum has no `renameFailed` — see the ordinals above.
function buildManagedFilePathOperation(module StdModule, name ByteArray, mode ManagedFilePathOpMode, fallbackFlag ParsedInt)
	var paramCount = 1 as ParamCount
	var paramTypes = oneI64Param()
	if managedFilePathOpTakesTwoPaths(mode) 'twoPaths'
		paramCount = 2 as ParamCount
		paramTypes = twoI64Params()
	end 'twoPaths'
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: paramCount, paramTypes: paramTypes, returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: managedFileThrows())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(paramCount)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var afterFirst = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfterm")
	var afterSecond = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfterm2")
	var failed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mffail")
	var ok = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfok")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: paramCount)
	let firstPath = emitPathToCString(module, func: func, cur: entry, join: afterFirst, pathRec: 0 as ValueId, ids: ids)
	// The second path's copy runs from `afterFirst` into `afterSecond`; a one-path operation simply falls
	// through, so the two blocks stay in the same order and neither builder needs its own skeleton.
	let secondPath = emitOptionalSecondPath(module, func: func, cur: afterFirst, join: afterSecond, mode: mode, ids: ids)

	let verdict = ids.mint()
	emitIoPark(module, block: afterSecond, operation: GtIoOperation.fileExists, ids: ids)
	match mode 'op'
		delete then module.appendOp(afterSecond, op: StdOp.osFileDelete(verdict, pathPtrId: firstPath))
		rename then module.appendOp(afterSecond, op: StdOp.osFileRename(verdict, oldPathPtrId: firstPath, newPathPtrId: secondPath))
	end 'op'
	let err = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterSecond, op: StdOp.osLastError(err))
	emitRawFree(module, block: afterSecond, ptr: firstPath, ids: ids)
	if managedFilePathOpTakesTwoPaths(mode) 'freeSecond'
		emitRawFree(module, block: afterSecond, ptr: secondPath, ids: ids)
	end 'freeSecond'
	let falseConst = emitConst(module, block: afterSecond, value: Win32False, ids: ids)
	let failedCall = emitCmp(module, block: afterSecond, a: verdict, b: falseConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterSecond, op: StdOp.condBranch(failedCall, thenBlockId: failed.id, elseBlockId: ok.id))

	emitFileErrnoClassifiedThrow(module, func: func, block: failed, err: err, fallbackFlag: fallbackFlag, ids: ids)
	emitVoidOkReturn(module, block: ok, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedFilePathOperation'

// Which path-taking operation a `buildManagedFilePathOperation` is emitting.
enum ManagedFilePathOpMode
	delete
	rename
end 'ManagedFilePathOpMode'

function managedFilePathOpTakesTwoPaths(mode ManagedFilePathOpMode) returns bool
	return match mode 'arity'
		delete gives false
		rename gives true
	end 'arity'
end 'managedFilePathOpTakesTwoPaths'

// The second path's scratch copy, for the operation that has one. A one-path operation still needs `cur`
// wired to `join` (the skeleton above carries on from `join` either way), and returns a value nothing
// reads — which is the honest shape here rather than an optional: the `rename` arm is the only reader and
// `managedFilePathOpTakesTwoPaths` gates it.
function emitOptionalSecondPath(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, join IrBlock, mode ManagedFilePathOpMode, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	if not managedFilePathOpTakesTwoPaths(mode) 'onePath'
		module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(join.id))
		return emitConst(module, block: join, value: 0, ids: ids)
	end 'onePath'
	// Parameter 1 — the SECOND path. `emitParams` numbers a function's parameters from 0, so the id IS the
	// index and needs no cast.
	let newPathRec = 1 as ValueId
	return emitPathToCString(module, func: func, cur: cur, join: join, pathRec: newPathRec, ids: ids)
end 'emitOptionalSecondPath'

// `__mf_stat(pathRec) -> (buffer, flag)` — the 48-byte metadata buffer, or `statFailed`/an errno variant.
//
// ⚠ **THE CALLER OWNS THE BUFFER AND MUST `statFree` IT.** It is a raw `__mm_alloc` block handed back as a
// bare `int`, so the ownership model cannot see it and the exit-101 leak gate WILL: a `stat` whose buffer
// is never freed fails the suite, which is exactly the behaviour wanted.
function buildManagedFileStat(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedFileStatName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: managedFileThrows())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var afterCopy = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfterm")
	var failed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mffail")
	var pack = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfpack")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let pathRec = 0 as ValueId

	let scratch = emitPathToCString(module, func: func, cur: entry, join: afterCopy, pathRec: pathRec, ids: ids)
	let dataSize = emitConst(module, block: afterCopy, value: FindDataBytes, ids: ids)
	let findData = emitRawAlloc(module, block: afterCopy, byteCount: dataSize, ids: ids)
	let search = ids.mint()
	emitIoPark(module, block: afterCopy, operation: GtIoOperation.fileExists, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.osFileFindFirst(search, pathPtrId: scratch, findDataPtrId: findData))
	let err = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.osLastError(err))
	emitRawFree(module, block: afterCopy, ptr: scratch, ids: ids)
	let invalidConst = emitConst(module, block: afterCopy, value: InvalidHandleValue, ids: ids)
	let notFound = emitCmp(module, block: afterCopy, a: search, b: invalidConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterCopy, op: StdOp.condBranch(notFound, thenBlockId: failed.id, elseBlockId: pack.id))

	// The find record is released on the failure path too — the throw carries no buffer, so nothing else
	// ever will.
	emitRawFree(module, block: failed, ptr: findData, ids: ids)
	emitFileErrnoClassifiedThrow(module, func: func, block: failed, err: err, fallbackFlag: ManagedFileStatFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(pack, op: StdOp.osFileFindClose(search))
	let bufSize = emitConst(module, block: pack, value: StatBufferBytes, ids: ids)
	let out = emitRawAlloc(module, block: pack, byteCount: bufSize, ids: ids)

	// [0] size = (nFileSizeHigh << 32) | nFileSizeLow — two DWORD loads, so both zero-extend.
	let sizeHigh = emitLoadDword(module, block: pack, record: findData, offset: FindDataSizeHighOffset, ids: ids)
	let shiftConst = emitConst(module, block: pack, value: FileSizeHighShift, ids: ids)
	let highPart = emitBin(module, block: pack, a: sizeHigh, b: shiftConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	let sizeLow = emitLoadDword(module, block: pack, record: findData, offset: FindDataSizeLowOffset, ids: ids)
	let totalSize = emitBin(module, block: pack, a: highPart, b: sizeLow, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: pack, record: out, offset: StatSizeOffset, value: totalSize)

	// [8]/[16]/[24] the three timestamps, FILETIME → Unix seconds. The ORDER of the three stat slots is the
	// bootstrap's (modified, created, accessed) and is NOT the order of the fields in the find record.
	let modified = emitFileTimeToUnixSeconds(module, block: pack, findData: findData, offset: FindDataLastWriteTimeOffset, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: pack, record: out, offset: StatModifiedOffset, value: modified)
	let created = emitFileTimeToUnixSeconds(module, block: pack, findData: findData, offset: FindDataCreationTimeOffset, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: pack, record: out, offset: StatCreatedOffset, value: created)
	let accessed = emitFileTimeToUnixSeconds(module, block: pack, findData: findData, offset: FindDataLastAccessTimeOffset, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: pack, record: out, offset: StatAccessedOffset, value: accessed)

	// [32]/[40] the two attribute BITS, published as 0 or 1 — never the raw mask.
	let attrs = emitLoadDword(module, block: pack, record: findData, offset: FindDataAttributesOffset, ids: ids)
	var afterDir = emitStoreAttributeFlag(module, func: func, cur: pack, out: out, offset: StatIsDirectoryOffset, attrs: attrs, mask: FileAttributeDirectory, ids: ids)
	var afterReadOnly = emitStoreAttributeFlag(module, func: func, cur: afterDir, out: out, offset: StatIsReadOnlyOffset, attrs: attrs, mask: FileAttributeReadOnly, ids: ids)

	emitRawFree(module, block: afterReadOnly, ptr: findData, ids: ids)
	emitOkReturn(module, block: afterReadOnly, value: out, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedFileStat'

// One unsigned DWORD out of a record — see `DwordMask` for why it is a word load and a mask rather than a
// 4-byte access.
//
// ⚠ **EXPORTED WHEN THE SOCKET FAMILY BECAME THE FIRST SECOND CALLER THAT REALLY LOADS ONE.** R4.3 exported
// it for the directory family and took it back: `__md_exists` masks a value `GetFileAttributesA` returned in
// a REGISTER, which is `DwordMask` and no load at all. `__ms_tcp_connect` reads `sin_addr` — a genuine
// 4-byte FIELD inside a `sockaddr` the OS filled — so it needs exactly this pair, and a second copy would be
// free to disagree about whether the mask or the load came first.
export function emitLoadDword(module StdModule, block IrBlock, record ValueId, offset ByteOffset, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let word = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: record, offset: offset, ids: ids)
	let mask = emitConst(module, block: block, value: DwordMask, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: word, b: mask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
end 'emitLoadDword'

// `(FILETIME - epochDelta) / ticksPerSecond` — the three timestamps' shared arithmetic. The load is
// UNALIGNED (the find record's three FILETIMEs sit at 4/12/20 behind a leading DWORD), which x64 takes
// without ceremony.
function emitFileTimeToUnixSeconds(module StdModule, block IrBlock, findData ValueId, offset ByteOffset, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let ft = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(ft, addrId: findData, offset: offset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let epoch = emitConst(module, block: block, value: FileTimeUnixEpochDelta, ids: ids)
	let sinceEpoch = emitBin(module, block: block, a: ft, b: epoch, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let ticks = emitConst(module, block: block, value: FileTimeTicksPerSecond, ids: ids)
	let seconds = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.div(seconds, lhs: sinceEpoch, rhs: ticks, signedness: DivideSignedness.signed))
	return seconds
end 'emitFileTimeToUnixSeconds'

// Store `(attrs and mask) != 0` as 0 or 1 at `out + offset`, and return the block to carry on in. Two
// return blocks because there is no `setcc` in this tier — the shape `MmRuntime.emitConstReturn` states once
// for every runtime predicate, and for the same reason.
function emitStoreAttributeFlag(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, out ValueId, offset ByteOffset, attrs ValueId, mask ParsedInt, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var setBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfattr1")
	var clearBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfattr0")
	let cont = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfattrc")

	let maskConst = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: mask, ids: ids)
	let bit = emitBin(module, block: cur, a: attrs, b: maskConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	let noBit = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let isSet = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: bit, b: noBit, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(isSet, thenBlockId: setBlock.id, elseBlockId: clearBlock.id))

	let one = emitConst(module, block: setBlock, value: 1, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: setBlock, record: out, offset: offset, value: one)
	module.setTerminator(setBlock, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))

	let zero = emitConst(module, block: clearBlock, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: clearBlock, record: out, offset: offset, value: zero)
	module.setTerminator(clearBlock, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))

	return cont
end 'emitStoreAttributeFlag'

// `__mf_stat_field(buffer, index) -> i64` — read field `index` of a `stat` buffer. NON-throwing: both
// references register it that way because a null buffer or an index past the six fields is an INVARIANT
// violation, not a runtime condition, and there is no error a caller could act on.
//
// ⚠ **IT ABORTS RATHER THAN READING PAST THE BUFFER.** Without the two guards a `statField(buf, 99)`
// returns whatever sits 792 bytes past a 48-byte allocation — a silent wrong answer, and the exact class
// this rung may not ship. `RuntimeAbort` owns the exit codes.
function buildManagedFileStatField(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedFileStatFieldName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var checkIndex = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfchki")
	var badBuffer = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfbadbuf")
	var badIndex = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfbadidx")
	var load = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfload")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let buffer = 0 as ValueId
	let index = 1 as ValueId

	let nullConst = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let isNull = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: buffer, b: nullConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isNull, thenBlockId: badBuffer.id, elseBlockId: checkIndex.id))
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: badBuffer, abort: RuntimeAbort.managedFileStatNullBuffer, ids: ids)

	// BOTH ENDS, in two compares: `StdCmpPred` is SIGNED throughout this tier (there is no unsigned
	// predicate), so a lone `index >= fields` accepts a negative index and reads BELOW the buffer. This is
	// `ManagedMemoryRuntime.emitIndexOutOfRange`'s shape and it is here for its reason.
	var checkHigh = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfchkhi")
	let zeroIndex = emitConst(module, block: checkIndex, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let negativeIndex = emitCmp(module, block: checkIndex, a: index, b: zeroIndex, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkIndex, op: StdOp.condBranch(negativeIndex, thenBlockId: badIndex.id, elseBlockId: checkHigh.id))

	let fieldsConst = emitConst(module, block: checkHigh, value: StatBufferFields, ids: ids)
	let pastEnd = emitCmp(module, block: checkHigh, a: index, b: fieldsConst, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkHigh, op: StdOp.condBranch(pastEnd, thenBlockId: badIndex.id, elseBlockId: load.id))
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: badIndex, abort: RuntimeAbort.managedFileStatIndexOutOfRange, ids: ids)

	let strideConst = emitConst(module, block: load, value: StatFieldBytes, ids: ids)
	let byteOffset = emitBin(module, block: load, a: index, b: strideConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let slot = emitBin(module, block: load, a: buffer, b: byteOffset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let value = emitLoadField(module, block: load, record: slot, offset: 0 as ByteOffset, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(load, op: StdOp.ret(value))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedFileStatField'

// `__mf_stat_free(buffer)` — release a `stat` buffer. NON-throwing, and it ABORTS on a null pointer for
// the reason `statField` does: `__mm_free` is not null-safe, so the alternative is a wild write to the
// header eight bytes below address 0.
function buildManagedFileStatFree(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedFileStatFreeName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var badBuffer = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfbadbuf")
	var release = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mfrel")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let buffer = 0 as ValueId

	let nullConst = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let isNull = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: buffer, b: nullConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isNull, thenBlockId: badBuffer.id, elseBlockId: release.id))
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: badBuffer, abort: RuntimeAbort.managedFileStatNullBuffer, ids: ids)

	emitRawFree(module, block: release, ptr: buffer, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(release, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedFileStatFree'

// The name this family's ordinals are transcribed under, for `BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck`'s diagnostic — one
// constant so the lookup's message and the claim's message name the same author.
let ManagedFileOrdinalInstaller = b"the __ManagedFile runtime"

// ⭐ **THE ORDINAL DRIFT GUARD FOR `__ManagedFileError` (7b, re-anchored at A1s)** — the enum's case ORDER is
// this family's wire format and the ordinals at the top of this file are transcribed literals of it, so the
// two are asserted to agree on every compile. Drift is otherwise SILENT: every handler arm would name the
// wrong case, with no crash and no wrong exit code.
//
// ⚠ **THE DECLARATION IT VERIFIES AGAINST IS `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF A1s.** It
// used to be a `Project.builtinManagedFileErrorEnum()` the compiler built — a second transcription of the
// same enum, which the parsed declaration then displaced in both registries anyway, so the guard verified a
// layout no handler arm was selected from. See `Project.BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck`'s header for the
// abort-instead-of-42 measurement that shows.
//
// ⚠ It is a FREE function rather than the head of `installManagedFileRuntime` because it now needs the
// `Project` — both to read the declaration and to report E4016 — and because it must run whether or not the
// program touches a file. `Compiler.compileToCodeResult` calls it beside its four siblings, ahead of the
// pipeline's error gate.
export function verifyManagedFileOrdinals(project Project)
	match declaredBuiltinErrorEnum(project, typeName: ManagedFileErrorTypeName, installer: ManagedFileOrdinalInstaller) 'declaration'
		notDeclared then return
		found(declaration) then claimManagedFileOrdinals(project, declaration: declaration)
	end 'declaration'
end 'verifyManagedFileOrdinals'

function claimManagedFileOrdinals(project Project, declaration EnumLayout)
	var fileErrors = BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck.overDeclared(project, declaration: declaration, installer: ManagedFileOrdinalInstaller)
	fileErrors.claim(ManagedFileNotFoundCaseName, ordinal: ManagedFileNotFoundOrdinal)
	fileErrors.claim(ManagedFileAccessDeniedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedFileAccessDeniedOrdinal)
	fileErrors.claim(ManagedFileOpenFailedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedFileOpenFailedOrdinal)
	fileErrors.claim(ManagedFileReadFailedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedFileReadFailedOrdinal)
	fileErrors.claim(ManagedFileWriteFailedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedFileWriteFailedOrdinal)
	fileErrors.claim(ManagedFileSizeFailedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedFileSizeFailedOrdinal)
	fileErrors.claim(ManagedFileDeleteFailedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedFileDeleteFailedOrdinal)
	fileErrors.claim(ManagedFileStatFailedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedFileStatFailedOrdinal)
	// The two the runtime ABORTS on rather than throwing — see the ordinal block at the top of this file.
	fileErrors.neverThrown(ManagedFileInvalidStatBufferCaseName, ordinal: ManagedFileInvalidStatBufferOrdinal)
	fileErrors.neverThrown(ManagedFileInvalidStatIndexCaseName, ordinal: ManagedFileInvalidStatIndexOrdinal)
	fileErrors.claim(ManagedFileClosedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedFileClosedOrdinal)
	fileErrors.requireTotal()
end 'claimManagedFileOrdinals'

// ⭐ **INSTALL + THE BIDIRECTIONAL DRIFT GUARD**, copied from `installManagedMemoryRuntime` for its stated reason:
// the predicate above and the `throws` clauses stamped by the builders are ONE fact in two places, and a
// builder that gains or loses a clause without the predicate following is a `try` that silently stops
// being required (or an E3057 on a call that cannot fail). Checked in BOTH directions.
export function installManagedFileRuntime(module StdModule, usage RuntimeUsage)
	if not usage.usesManagedFile 'noManagedFile'
		return
	end 'noManagedFile'

	buildManagedFileOpen(module, name: ManagedFileOpenReadName, mode: ManagedFileOpenMode.read)
	buildManagedFileOpen(module, name: ManagedFileOpenWriteName, mode: ManagedFileOpenMode.write)
	buildManagedFileClose(module)
	buildManagedFileDestruct(module)
	buildManagedFileSize(module)
	buildManagedFileRead(module)
	buildManagedFileWrite(module)
	buildManagedFileExists(module)
	buildManagedFilePathOperation(module, name: ManagedFileDeleteName, mode: ManagedFilePathOpMode.delete, fallbackFlag: ManagedFileDeleteFailedFlag)
	// ⚠ `rename`'s catch-all is `deleteFailed`, not a variant of its own — both references do this and the
	// enum has no `renameFailed` to reach for. See the ordinals at the top of this file.
	buildManagedFilePathOperation(module, name: ManagedFileRenameName, mode: ManagedFilePathOpMode.rename, fallbackFlag: ManagedFileDeleteFailedFlag)
	buildManagedFileStat(module)
	buildManagedFileStatField(module)
	buildManagedFileStatFree(module)

	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		if not isManagedFileRuntimeCallee(func.name) 'notOurs'
			continue
		end 'notOurs'
		let named = isThrowingManagedFileRuntimeCallee(func.name)
		let stamped = throwsClauseIsThrowing(func.throwsTypeName)
		if stamped and not named 'stampedNotNamed'
			panic("installManagedFileRuntime: '{String.from(func.name)}' stamps a `throws` clause but isThrowingManagedFileRuntimeCallee does NOT name it — the throwing-builder set and the predicate have drifted (R4.1)")
		end 'stampedNotNamed'
		if named and not stamped 'namedNotStamped'
			panic("installManagedFileRuntime: isThrowingManagedFileRuntimeCallee names '{String.from(func.name)}' as throwing but its builder stamps NO `throws` clause — the throwing-builder set and the predicate have drifted (R4.1)")
		end 'namedNotStamped'

		// ⭐ **THE THIRD LIST (D12 review): a throwing callee must also have a SOURCE-METHOD arm.** Asked, not
		// described — the answer is discarded and the CALL is the check, so a missing arm is
		// `managedFileSourceMethod`'s own panic here at install rather than in front of a user who forgot a
		// `try` on that one callee. `GtRuntime.runtimeCalleeSourceMethod`'s header holds the whole reasoning,
		// including why there is no backward arm and no column of names.
		// ⚠ This family's map answers the UNION (it owns the one non-injective callee), so the arms are named
		// here rather than an answer being read as a name. `notFound` is unreachable — the map panics before
		// it — and is a `panic` rather than a `break` for that reason: reaching it would mean the map grew a
		// silent absent-state, which is what the union's `notFound` means for a family with NO map at all.
		if named 'hasSourceMethod'
			match managedFileSourceMethod(func.name) 'spelling'
				found(method) then requireQuotableSourceSpelling(method, callee: func.name)
				sharedByTwoSpellings(spelling, alsoSpelled) then requireQuotableSharedSpellings(spelling, alsoSpelled: alsoSpelled, callee: func.name)
				notFound then panic("installManagedFileRuntime: `managedFileSourceMethod` answers notFound for '{String.from(func.name)}', which isThrowingManagedFileRuntimeCallee names as throwing — every throwing `__mf_` callee must have a source-method arm, and this map may not have an absent state")
			end 'spelling'
		end 'hasSourceMethod'
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'installManagedFileRuntime'

// Every callee this file installs — what the drift guard iterates, what `scanRuntimeUsage` reads to set
// `usesManagedFile`, and what `SemanticCheck.calleeNeedsWin32Substrate` refuses on a non-Windows target.
// A PREFIX test rather than a thirteen-way `or`: every entry point this file installs is `__mf_`-named by
// construction, and a fourteenth would otherwise have to be remembered here as well as declared above.
export function isManagedFileRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: ManagedFileRuntimePrefix)
end 'isManagedFileRuntimeCallee'
